s even now our offices aren't  fancy by any stretch of the imagination  but back then my office was above a  tea shop in downtown Mountain View and  it was kind of you know one of our first  funders walked in it's a salad this is  not a this is not a healthy working  environment and so she she sent some  painters and handyman to just make it a  vaguely decent environment but George  Apollo visited that office and you you  know if you're from Brazil you might  have heard of George Apollo III I'm  ashamed to say that I didn't know about  George Apollo but he's actually one of  the biggest philanthropist in Brazil and  he and his team came and said we need to  bring Khan Academy to Brazil as soon as  possible and we were like a five person  organization at the time they were like  oh well we don't know how we're gonna be  able to do that and they're like well we  can support Khan Academy we can help  support the efforts in Brazil and so the  lemon foundation named after George  Apollo lemon  they helped support Khan Academy proper  and so they've since then been great  donors and supporters of us but they've  also we've been able to partner with  them in Brazil to align our to localize  the work into Brazilian Portuguese align  it with the Brazilian national Common  Core do efficacy in research studies in  Brazilian schools there was even a soap  opera episode in Brazil I heard where  the protagonist I ran a fundraiser for  Khan Academy so we have a lot of good  connections to Brazil I visited there  and I had a wonderful time I actually  brought my whole family there and and  and worked with the lemon folks so  that's what really pulled us in to  Brazil so we have 45 translation  projects around the world most of its  volunteers who are able to do it in  their own languages and their unpaid and  we and your donations are allowing us to  build a platform so that they can do it  but there's several geographies that  were able to go deeper Brazil is one of  them  India's and other and then  spanish-speaking Latin America  especially places like Peru and Mexico  we have partners Carlos Slim Foundation  in Mexico and Carlos Rodriguez pastor  and his foundation in Peru and Tata  trust in India these are all foundations  that have allowed us to go deeper but I  do want to make it clear we are all of  these groups have each been donating a  lot of resources to make this happen but  for all of us to reach literally the  hundreds of millions of billions we need  more resources and so it's it's a real  partnership with both large philanthropy  and foundations and  small philanthropists oh and people who  can give even that $20 at $10 or even  even those $3 it all makes a huge  difference so a question from youtube  emanuel so its asks how important do you  think coding will be for children's  education in the future so I'm a big  coding fan my background my undergrad I  was a computer science major I always  got a lot of joy out of coding coding  empowered me to build the first versions  of Khan Academy kind of Kennedy wouldn't  exist if I didn't have some of those  early desires to prototype with coding  and so I view it as it can be a very  very valuable skill that's why we've  invested on Khan Academy on our coding  platform we're kids and I encourage  anyone anyone who thinks that coding is  not for them go to Khan Academy and try  our coding platform you're going to see  that it focuses on the creative side of  coding you get to make pictures and  animations literally within minutes but  it takes you all the way down the path  so that you meet you can actually  understand fairly sophisticated ideas in  computer programming and computer  science and so I think there's two  tracks I think there's a lot of you know  the need for software engineers computer  programmers it's only going to increase  there's so many industries that are  going to be are going to leverage things  like computing and data analytics and  data science and artificial intelligence  more than they are today  and so there's just going to be more and  more and more jobs there so it's  valuable for that but even if someone  isn't going to become a computer  programmer if you're going to become a  doctor if you're gonna become a lawyer  whatever feel accountant whatever field  someone goes in almost every one of  those industries are going to be  affected by computing going forward and  so it's valuable to have at least a  baseline understanding of what computers  can do how computing logic works so that  you can navigate whatever field you go  into in the future I'll also add that  you know the what's fun about  programming and computing is it helps  build a certain form of critical  thinking and logic which is I think  valuable in every field so a big picture  you know I'm still a big you know you're  reading you're reading you're three  hours so to speak which were never ours  three hours two hours and an a a reading  riting and rithmetic those are always  going to be super core and there's  be super core for the 21st century and  but you know I think computing builds a  lot of those same skills that the  mathematics does in terms of the  critical thinking and and there's a  whole there's of it there's a creation  side to it too in this engineering side  which is super valuable so if kids can  get exposed to it I would love to see it  and that's why we we have that on Khan  Academy we also have APCs principals  which is an Advanced Placement course  which is designed to be broad so that a  lot of people might want to take it so  you could go on Khan Academy right now  and are you could either learn to  program on our coding platform or go to  APCs principles and I think almost  anyone watching this could engage on it  and it not only does it do the basics of  coding it is how do computers work how  does the internet work what is internet  security all of these things you are  always hearing about we make available  on on Khan Academy so I definitely  recommend taking a look at some of that  work as well so let's see there's a  question there's a testimonial from AJ  who's a student so AJ wrote to us and  says no joke the only reason I graduated  college was because of Khan Academy I  pledge to myself that if I graduated I  would give every year after I graduated  I have kept that promise and I am giving  a little bit more during the kovat  pandemic because I want to make sure  that everyone has the opportunity to  learn well AJ thank you so much that  means a ton to us I know especially for  a young professional you're out of  college you probably don't have a lot of  money but whatever you can donate makes  a big difference you know makes a  difference on two levels though the  money obviously allows us to do work  allows us to hire people pay our server  costs our server costs our loan or  several million dollars a year but also  just that show of confidence you know I  can't tell you you know that positive  energy that you send our way by  supporting this makes a big difference  to us as well it energizes me and I know  it energizes the team and it's it's a  very powerful example for hopefully  other people to help donate to this this  cause that I that we think can can  empower billions of folks over  generations so from YouTube life an ask  Sal if you have 1 million dollars what  will you do with it and I'm assuming the  question is if Khan Academy had a  million dollar  if I had a million dollars I might think  about donating it to Khan Academy  you know I'll answer your question I'll  go on a little tangent a lot of people  said Sal how come Khan Academy isn't a  for-profit company and you know I went  through that thought process in the  early days cuz before I actually even  talk to any philanthropist I live in the  middle of Silicon Valley some of my  friends are venture capitalists they  offered hey I'll write $100,000 check  right now you give me X percent and then  you can qu